# AGENTS.md

Context for AI coding agents working in this repository.

## Project

{{ project_name }} — a FastAPI REST API generated by [brupy](https://github.com/abdulazeezoj/brupy).

## Setup

```
uv sync
```

## Run

```
uv run fastapi dev src/{{ package_name }}/main.py
```
{% if migrations %}
Apply migrations first: `uv run alembic upgrade head`.
{% endif %}
{% if worker == "taskiq" %}
Run the worker (separate process): `uv run taskiq worker {{ package_name }}.worker:broker --app-dir src`.
{% elif worker == "celery" %}
Run the worker (separate process): `PYTHONPATH=src uv run celery -A {{ package_name }}.worker.celery_app worker --loglevel=info`.
{% endif %}

## Test

```
uv run pytest
```

## Layout

Two fixed entrypoints, two "add a resource here" folders, one folder for
shared infrastructure — the rest is unopinionated (borrowed from how
Next.js only enforces structure for routing, nothing else):

- `src/{{ package_name }}/main.py` — **the** FastAPI entrypoint: `app`,
  lifespan, mounted routers. Don't rename or move it.
- `src/{{ package_name }}/routes/` — **one module per HTTP resource.**
  `items.py` is the example; a new resource is a new file here plus one
  `app.include_router(...)` line in `main.py`.
{% if worker != "none" %}
- `src/{{ package_name }}/worker.py` — **the** {{ worker }} entrypoint:
  broker/app setup, importing every `tasks/` module so the worker
  process can discover them. Don't rename or move it.
- `src/{{ package_name }}/scheduler.py` — the {{ worker }} scheduler
  entrypoint, a separate process from `worker.py` that only decides
  *when* to enqueue a task; the worker still does the actual work.
- `src/{{ package_name }}/tasks/` — **one module per background job.**
  `example.py` is the demo; a new task is a new file here plus one
  import line in `worker.py` (see the comment there for why).
{% endif %}
- `src/{{ package_name }}/core/` — shared infrastructure every
  route/task might depend on: `config.py` (`Settings`, always), {% if database != "none" %}`db.py`
  (async engine/session), {% endif %}{% if redis %}`redis.py` (the `redis_client`), {% endif %}and
  the natural home for anything similar later. Add
  new config fields to `Settings`, not scattered `os.environ` reads.
- `src/{{ package_name }}/schemas/` — Pydantic request/response
  models, one module per resource. `items.py` is the example; a new
  resource is a new file here, re-exported from `__init__.py`.
{% if database != "none" %}
- `src/{{ package_name }}/models/` — {{ orm }} models, one module per
  resource. `items.py` is the example; a new resource is a new file
  here, re-exported from `__init__.py`.
{% endif %}
{% if migrations %}
- `alembic/` — migrations. New model → `uv run alembic revision
  --autogenerate -m "..."`, review the generated file, then `uv run
  alembic upgrade head`.
{% endif %}
- `scripts/` — one-off and operational scripts (data seeding,
  maintenance tasks) meant to be run directly rather than imported.
- `tests/` — pytest tests, split into `unit/` (no I/O), `e2e/`
  (full request flows){% if database != "none" %}, and `integration/`
  (persistence through the real database session){% endif %}{% if database != "none" %} — all
  against an isolated in-memory SQLite database via a `get_session`
  override in `tests/conftest.py`, which applies to the whole tree; the
  production database is never touched by the test suite{% endif %}.

## Conventions

- Dependencies are managed with `uv` via `pyproject.toml` — don't hand-edit
  `uv.lock`; run `uv add <package>` / `uv remove <package>` instead.
- Keep route handlers typed; return plain dicts or Pydantic models, not
  raw strings.
- New HTTP endpoints go in `routes/`, new background jobs go in
  `tasks/`, new shared infrastructure goes in `core/` — don't put any of
  these in `main.py`/`worker.py` themselves, which stay thin entrypoints.
{% if database != "none" %}
- New endpoints that touch the database take `session` via
  `Depends(get_session)` — don't create engines/sessions ad hoc.
{% endif %}

